Hi Dennis,

I'll be aware of your suggestions next time.  Thanks.

Your suggestions worked quite well.  The key to modifying what I had done to get it all to work was the addition of nesting atop inside of bquote.

  myplot<- subset(anon, species==i)
  my_title<-bquote(atop(paste("Mean Suitability for ", italic(.(i)), " by Year"), "Anonymous National Park"))

thanks so much for you help.  This will be a huge time saver.

Kris

On Thursday, December 17, 2015 at 9:20:42 PM UTC-6, Dennis Murphy wrote:
> Hi:
>
> 1. You need to simplify your plot call - the calls to theme() are
> unnecessary for the purposes of your question.
> 2. Your code got mashed together likely because you sent your mail in
> HTML; therefore, it wasn't easy to read.
>
> Complaints aside, the biggest programming problem was to combine
> normal and italic fonts in the title across two lines and avoid
> getting a large space between the end of the first text chunk and the
> italicized text. From your end, you have to distinguish the contents
> of an atomic vector from their respective indices/locations. Below is
> one way to do what I think you requested. I made up a fake data set to
> generate multiple species; you could extend the idea to species-park
> combinations if that is of interest.
>
>
> # Fake data set - year is numeric rather than factor, so a group
> # aesthetic for it is unnecessary.
> DF <- data.frame(
>        species = factor(rep(paste0("species_", LETTERS[1:3]), each = 10)),
>        rcp = c("group1", "group2"),
>        year = rep(2011:2015, each = 2),
>        MEAN = runif(30, 0.3, 0.6))
>
> library(ggplot2)
>
> # define an atomic vector of species names
> species_names <- as.character(unique(DF$species))
>
> # A function to create a plot for an individual species. It takes a
> # data frame DF and a character object species_name as formal arguments
>
> plotfun <- function(DF, species_name)
> {
>    d <- DF[DF$species == species_name, ]   # get the data subset
>
>    # Manufacture an expression that creates a two-line title
>    my_title <- bquote(atop(paste("Mean Suitability for ",
> italic(.(species_name)), " by Year"),
>                                "Anonymous National Park"))
>
>    # create and return the ggplot
>    ggplot(d, aes(x = year, y = MEAN, colour = rcp)) +
>       geom_point() +
>       geom_line() +
>       labs(title = my_title, x = "Projection Year",
>           y="Mean Suitability\nValue", colour = "factor")
> }
>
> # Apply the above function to each species, return a list of ggplot objects
> L <- lapply(species_names,
>             function(x) plotfun(DF, x))
>
> # Cycle through the list of plots and save them to individual files
> for(i in seq_along(species_names))
> {
>   filename <- paste(species_names[i], "png", sep = ".")
>   ggsave(filename, L[[i]], width = 6, height = 2.5)
> }
>
> Creating axis titles and main titles in a plot can sometimes be a
> challenge, particularly when one needs to insert non-standard text
> such as mathematics or text in a modified font. The challenge here was
> to create a main title with two lines that mixes normal and italic
> text. \n works when given a text string without 'embellishments', but
> unexpected things can happen if your input is an expression rather
> than a string. I made two adjustments: (i) I used atop() because it
> can take an expression or a string as an argument; (ii) I modified the
> title so that the italicized text was on the first line inside an
> expression().
>
> The code above cycles through each species name, first taking the
> appropriate data subset and then producing and returning a plot with a
> two-line main title. The lapply() call produces a list of plots, one
> per species. The loop at the end cycles through the list, saving each
> plot to a file in png format. I find it useful to save the plots in a
> list in case you need to reference them later. If you don't want the
> list of plots, assign the ggplot() output to an object, say p, inside
> plotfun() and make its last line
>
> ggsave(p, file = paste(species_name, "png", sep = ".", width = 6, height = 2.5))
>
>
> HTH,
> Dennis
>
